1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:02,020
StreamWell.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:03,412
Check the stream.

3
00:00:03,412 --> 00:00:04,640
Check yourself.

4
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:10,833
OneAquaHealth asks: do healthy urban streams make healthier people?

5
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:14,420
Today, nobody can answer that, for two reasons.

6
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:21,100
Volunteers drift away: in large citizen-science projects, only about a quarter ever come back.

7
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:28,080
And the human half is missing: a stream record says nothing about how the stream made anyone feel.

8
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,417
StreamWell solves both with one loop.

9
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:36,737
Every visit becomes two health checks, the stream’s and yours.

10
00:00:36,737 --> 00:00:44,260
The personal answer brings volunteers back, and every return visit adds the paired data OneAquaHealth needs.

11
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,433
Meet Ana.

12
00:00:47,433 --> 00:00:49,752
She walks to a stream in Coimbra.

13
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:59,380
Everything here is built on OneAquaHealth’s real data: all 106 research sites, the app’s own answer codes, and the project’s lab results.

14
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:07,120
She sees today’s safe-walk advisory, from live weather and the lab’s health-risk score.

15
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:13,874
Thirty seconds to check in, with the four feelings the OneAquaHealth app already asks.

16
00:01:13,874 --> 00:01:21,360
We ask them again after the visit, so each person is compared with themselves, which removes most of the bias in mood data.

17
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:35,300
Then the official stream check, redesigned for non-experts: plain words, pictures, and an honest "not sure" that never counts against the stream.

18
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,566
Then a second look.

19
00:01:49,566 --> 00:02:00,800
No black box: every check shows what it noticed, why it matters, and which data it used: her other answers, recent weather, and her photo, analysed on the phone.

20
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:04,767
The AI suggests.

21
00:02:04,767 --> 00:02:08,690
Ana decides, and her decision stays with the record.

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,305
Two health checks, side by side.

23
00:02:15,305 --> 00:02:21,571
The stream is Good, every point explained, and Ana left more restored than she arrived.

24
00:02:21,571 --> 00:02:24,020
No other stream tool records that.

25
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:27,000
Privacy is built in.

26
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,550
Her feelings stay on her phone unless she opts in, and researchers only see averages over at least five people.

27
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,179
This is the reason to come back.

28
00:02:38,179 --> 00:02:44,320
Not points or badges, but an answer that matters to her: which streams restore me most?

29
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:50,540
With thin data, it says "not clear yet", instead of overclaiming.

30
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:58,690
Missions send volunteers to streams nobody has checked for a month, so engagement fills the gaps in monitoring.

31
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:08,270
And it reaches health care: a doctor can prescribe stream walks, and follow the outcome with the WHO-5 well-being index.

32
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:16,065
For cities, one map brings together citizen reports, real lab results and live weather.

33
00:03:16,065 --> 00:03:20,983
These visits are simulated, because this paired dataset does not exist yet.

34
00:03:20,983 --> 00:03:24,000
Creating it is exactly what StreamWell is for.

35
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:32,220
Healthier streams, more restored people: OneAquaHealth’s central hypothesis, made measurable.

36
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:34,610
And it shows where to act.

37
00:03:34,610 --> 00:03:42,340
Feeling unsafe, bad smells and litter cost the most, and each one becomes a concrete fix for the city.

38
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:52,280
If a storm hits tonight, risky streams turn red, with safer ones nearby.

39
00:03:53,640 --> 00:04:02,520
It all speaks the language of health systems: HL7 FHIR, on the OneAquaHealth Implementation Guide, at two privacy levels.

40
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,090
Zero validation errors, ready for the project’s own infrastructure.

41
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:12,344
And we tested our own claims.

42
00:04:12,344 --> 00:04:19,784
In two hundred simulated pilots, the model recovered every planted effect, with only four percent false alarms.

43
00:04:19,784 --> 00:04:22,599
Zero accessibility issues on every screen.

44
00:04:22,599 --> 00:04:27,760
And a pre-registered pilot is ready for one summer in one OneAquaHealth city.

45
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:34,521
StreamWell works offline on any phone, needs no server, and costs almost nothing to run.

46
00:04:34,521 --> 00:04:35,735
Check the stream.

47
00:04:35,735 --> 00:04:36,805
Check yourself.

48
00:04:36,805 --> 00:04:37,519
Thank you.
